on incorrect items: if errors cluster on specific non-checklisted cases (indicating shared systematic blind spots), the panel's reliability is bounded by checklist coverage rather than enhanced by aggregation; if errors are idiosyncratic and uncorrelated, aggregation drives reliability above any single member's baseline.","counterconditions":["The checklist is incomplete relative to the task's discriminative features, and omitted features are systematically correlated with outcome determination.","Panel members' training is homogeneous, producing correlated errors rather than independent ones.","The single expert's intuition captures features omitted by the checklist, making the expert's errors orthogonal to the panel's shared blind spots.","The evaluation sample is dominated by cases the checklist handles well, masking the panel's systematic failures on uncovered cases."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist reduces variance by constraining the hypothesis space to articulable features, but it also introduces a shared bias: all panel members are trained on the same checklist, so their errors are correlated on any case whose discriminative feature lies outside the checklist's scope. A single expert's intuition may incorporate un-articulable pattern matching that captures these omitted features, making the expert's errors orthogonal to the panel's. For objectively verifiable outcomes, panel superiority holds only when aggregation can exploit independent errors. The single-evaluation discriminator is the covariance structure of panel errors: high within-panel agreement on incorrect answers reveals shared systematic blind spots (checklist compliance tracking the right answer only on cases the checklist happens to cover), while high within-panel disagreement on incorrect answers reveals idiosyncratic errors that aggregation can suppress.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_008 concerns calibration curves specifically; this claim generalizes to any within-evaluation metric","SRC_008's limitation is about measurement granularity; this claim is about identifiability of causal structure from observational data"],"overturn_conditions":["Demonstration that at least one observable within a single evaluation has different expected values under the two hypotheses","Proof that error covariance structure is not jointly compatible with both causal reliability and coincidental tracking","An argument that the problem's 'single evaluation' permits structural assumptions that render the hypotheses identifiable"],"shared_structure":["Single evaluation cannot distinguish calibration from coincidence because both hypotheses predict the same observations","The problem's constraint mirrors the limitation that calibration curves across difficulty levels cannot be computed when outcomes are binary within a single evaluation"],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["Any single-evaluation metric is consistent with both genuine reliability and coincidental tracking","The distinction requires either a second evaluation or ex-ante knowledge of the checklist's causal mechanism","The problem's demand is analogous to asking whether a single coin flip can distinguish a fair coin from a biased coin that happens to produce heads on this flip"]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between genuine reliability gain and mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer cannot be made within a single evaluation, because compliance and reliability are observationally equivalent when outcomes are the only ground truth. The problem's demand for a single-evaluation discriminator is formally unsatisfiable: any metric computed within one evaluation (including error covariance, calibration, or item-level analysis) is consistent with both hypotheses.","counterconditions":["A single evaluation contains sufficient information to distinguish causal reliability from coincidental tracking (which would require an argument that the two hypotheses have disjoint likelihoods given any possible observation)","The problem permits implicit use of distributional assumptions about the evaluation set that would allow inference from a single sample","The distinction can be made via internal consistency metrics alone, without any out-of-sample validation"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Within a single evaluation, every observable—accuracy, agreement, error patterns, calibration—is compatible with two generative models: (1) the checklist causally produces reliability by enabling effective aggregation of independent errors, or (2) the checklist is a proxy that correlates with the answer on this particular evaluation set but does not produce generalizable reliability. Without a second evaluation on a different distribution or holdout set, these hypotheses cannot be distinguished. The problem's constraint to a single evaluation removes the primary tool for distinguishing reliability from coincidence. This is a structural limitation, not a measurement problem: the information needed to separate the hypotheses is not present in the data.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SRC_006 frames this as whether tacit knowledge can be articulated at all; here the checklist is given as explicit, so the question is whether articulable features are sufficient for the task","Polanyi's concern is epistemic (can tacit knowledge be made explicit?); here the concern is empirical (does the checklist cover the discriminative features?)","The expert's intuition is not necessarily the same as Polanyi's tacit knowledge—it may be explicit knowledge applied rapidly"],"overturn_conditions":["Empirical demonstration that all discriminative features for the task are articulable